 "grammar": {
  "title": {
   "ko": "will / won't로 예측·제안·약속",
   "en": "'will / won't' for predictions, offers and promises"
  },
  "explain": {
   "ko": "will + 동사 원형은 예측, 즉석에서 하는 제안과 결정, 약속에 씁니다. 모든 주어에 같은 형태이고 줄여서 I'll, she'll로 씁니다. 부정은 will not = won't입니다. 제안을 할 때는 Shall I ~?도 많이 씁니다.",
   "en": "Use will + base verb for predictions, for offers and decisions made at the moment of speaking, and for promises. It is the same for every subject and is often shortened: I'll, she'll. The negative is will not = won't. 'Shall I …?' is a common way to make an offer."
  },
  "table": {
   "head": [
    "용도 / Use",
    "예문 / Example"
   ],
   "rows": [
    [
     "예측 / prediction",
     "It will be hot tomorrow."
    ],
    [
     "제안 / offer",
     "I'll carry your bag."
    ],
    [
     "약속 / promise",
     "I won't be late."
    ],
    [
     "의문 / question",
     "Will it rain tonight?"
    ],
    [
     "정중한 제안 / polite offer",
     "Shall I open the door?"
    ]
   ]
  },
  "notes": [
   {
    "ko": "will 뒤에는 동사 원형: He will come (✕ He will comes).",
    "en": "After will use the base verb: He will come (not He will comes)."
   },
   {
    "ko": "probably는 will 뒤, won't 앞에 둡니다: It will probably rain. / It probably won't rain.",
    "en": "'Probably' goes after will but before won't: It will probably rain. / It probably won't rain."
   },
   {
    "ko": "be going to는 이미 정한 계획, will은 지금 정한 결정에 씁니다.",
    "en": "Use be going to for plans already made and will for decisions made now."
   }
  ],
  "examples": [
   {
    "t": "I'll help you.",
    "ko": "제가 도와드릴게요."
   },
   {
    "t": "It will be hot tomorrow.",
    "ko": "내일은 더울 거예요."
   },
   {
    "t": "He will probably be late.",
    "ko": "그는 아마 늦을 거예요."
   },
   {
    "t": "I won't be late.",
    "ko": "저는 늦지 않을 거예요."
   },
   {
    "t": "Shall I open the door?",
    "ko": "제가 문을 열어 드릴까요?"
   }
  ],
